          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of activity is described for someone with COPD who gets breathless walking?",
          "answer": "The retrieved material does not say which specific activities are recommended for someone with COPD who gets breathless walking. It does explain why exercise matters. A COPD summary (*Staying active with COPD*) describes a cycle where breathlessness leads to avoiding activity, muscles weaken, and the same task then feels harder. It says that exercise is treated as a therapy for COPD, and that most of the available improvement comes from breaking this cycle. The same summary says the kind of activity and how to keep it going are covered in a part of the document that was not returned, so I can't tell you what that part says.\n\nA general physical-activity summary (*Physical activity and anxiety*) describes moderate activity such as brisk walking, cycling or swimming, at a pace that raises breathing but still allows conversation. That guidance is written for anxiety, not COPD, so it may not apply in the same way.\n\nBecause you describe breathlessness on walking, a clinician or respiratory team is the right person to ask which activity and intensity suit you. Pulmonary rehabilitation, a structured exercise programme for people with lung conditions, is mentioned in the COPD material, but the retrieved text does not describe it.\n\nSeek urgent medical help if breathlessness gets worse, comes on at rest, or doesn't improve with your usual reliever. Also seek urgent help for confusion or unusual drowsiness, blue lips or fingers, chest pain, coughing up blood, or fever with a marked change in phlegm. In an emergency, call your local emergency number.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
